<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chalon-sur-Saône</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cabillonum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chalon-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on the river Saône. As the Roman Cabillonum it was a river port and later a bishopric, and in the 6th century the Merovingian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntram">Guntram</a> made it the capital of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a> of Burgundy. It later belonged to the Duchy of Burgundy. Nicéphore Niépce, the inventor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">photography</a>, was born there in 1765.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chalon-sur-saone.jpg" alt="Chalon-sur-Saône" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chalon-sur-Sa%C3%B4ne_-_Place_Saint-Vincent_-_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chalon-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> lies on the Saône in the south of the region of Bourgogne-Franche-Comté, about 55 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macon">Mâcon</a>. It is the largest city of the Saône-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> department, though the department's capital is the smaller Mâcon. It was once a busy port and distribution point for local wines, which were sent up and down the Saône and along the Canal du Centre, opened in 1792.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The site, ancient Cabillonum, was a capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a>, and objects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture</a> have been recovered from the river bed there. Its first mention is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commentarii-de-bello-gallico">Commentarii de Bello Gallico</a>. The Roman city was already a river port and a hub of road communications, including the Via Agrippa.</p><p>In 354 CE the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> stationed the Roman 7th Army there for an invasion of the lands of the brother kings Gundomadus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vadomarius">Vadomarius</a>, but the troops, not receiving supplies, revolted and were pacified with money by the grand chamberlain Eusebius. By Late Antiquity the city had shrunk so far that its wall enclosed fifteen hectares.</p><p>Saint Marcel is said to have been martyred there in 179. Chalon became one of the de facto capitals of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntram">Guntram</a>, king from 561 to 592, who died there and promoted the cult of Marcel. The city was frequently attacked until the 10th century. A diocese, a suffragan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, was established in the 6th century, and a church council met there from 644 to 655. After the Concordat of 1801 the diocese was merged with that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Autun</a>.</p><p>In the 19th century Nicéphore Niépce, born in the city in 1765, made the earliest surviving photograph; his 1807 Pyréolophore is probably the world's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/internal-combustion-engine">internal combustion engine</a>, and in 1818 he built a dandy horse, for which he coined the word vélocipède. Dominique Vivant Denon, who helped convert the royal palace into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> museum, also lived there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Hôtel de Ville was completed in 1845, and in the late 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and iron works were the main industry, among them the Petit-Creusot engineering works. Saint Vincent's Cathedral dates mainly from the 12th to the 15th centuries, with elements from the eighth century and a neo-Gothic façade of the 19th. The city has a museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">photography</a> on the Quai des Messageries, with more than two million photographs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chalon-sur-Saône” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Burgundy took shape in the late ninth and early tenth centuries in the western part of the old Burgundian lands, the region of Autun, Dijon, Beaune and <b>Chalon-sur-Saône</b> that remained within West Francia when Provence and Upper Burgundy formed separate kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the division of 561 Chlothar I's son Guntram received Burgundy with Orléans, ruling from <b>Chalon-sur-Saône</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/frankish-kingdom-of-burgundy">Frankish Kingdom of Burgundy</a></li></ul></section>
